``` Introduction

A field has to be declared inside a type's own body before anything outside can write to it. Plain `.` access from the outside can never create a new field -- it can only read or write one that already exists:
```
Castaway {
	name := 'unknown'
}

survivor := Castaway()
survivor.name = 'Jack'      # fine -- name already exists
@puts "survivor.name: `survivor.name`"   # survivor.name: Jack

# survivor.number = 4       -- raises Ore::Cannot_Assign_Undeclared_Identifier, number was never declared


``` Self-declaring with ./ is legitimate -- but only during construction

`./field := value` inside a type's own method self-declares a brand new field, same as writing `field := value` in the class body directly. That's allowed in the class body itself and inside `new{;}` (and anything `new{;}` calls) -- while the instance is still being built:
```
Raft {
	new {; ./planks := 6 }
}

r := Raft()
@puts "r.planks: `r.planks`"   # r.planks: 6 -- ./planks was self-declared during construction


``` A later method can't grow the instance's shape

Once construction finishes, self-declaring a brand new field the same way -- from some OTHER method, called after `new{;}` has already returned -- raises the same error external `.` access would. An instance's shape is locked in once it's built:
```
Raft2 {
	new {; ./planks := 6 }

	add_sail {;
		./sail := true   # too late -- construction already finished so ./sail cannot be declared on self here
	}
}

r2 := Raft2()
# r2.add_sail() would raise Ore::Cannot_Assign_Undeclared_Identifier
@puts "r2.planks: `r2.planks`"   # r2.planks: 6 -- the field declared during construction is still fine to use
